From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 09:59:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] script: Keep media elements alive while they can still play

Nothing kept a media element alive while it loaded or played: the player
only holds a weak reference to it. An element that script no longer
referenced was collected at the next garbage collection, which stopped
its playback, and a detached element that delayed the load event let the
load event fire early. The specification requires that "media elements
must not stop playing just because all references to them have been
removed".

Like Gecko, and Blink apart from autoplay, keep a media element alive
while it delays the load event or seeks, and, until it has ended
playback, while it plays or waits for data to play or is eligible for
autoplay. Its owner document holds such elements, so they never outlive
the document.

Fork note: meant for upstream; found with the QuickJS backend, which
frees such elements as soon as script drops them (the media tests have
failed on QuickJS since patch 61's pool scopes), but SpiderMonkey loses
them too once a GC runs (the new test times out without this change).
Besides the upstream change, this drops the QuickJS conditions in the
expectations of autoplay-disabled-by-permissions-policy; patch 72 drops
the others. Drop once upstream has it.

Testing: Adds a WPT test that unreferenced media elements keep playing
across a garbage collection; it times out without this change.

Validation: Release builds of upstream f040186a7ee with SpiderMonkey, with and without the series, `./mach test-wpt --processes 4` of html/semantics/embedded-content/media-elements, the-video-element, the-audio-element, infrastructure/assumptions/allowed-to-play.html, webaudio/the-audio-api/the-mediaelementaudiosourcenode-interface and 2 _mozilla media tests: the new test passes 7 of 7 (harness timeout without), autoplay-disabled-by-permissions-policy and played-loop as in the updated expectations (autoplay-disabled timed out intermittently before, upstream issues #46012 and #46804); the remaining differences are track timeouts and track-cues-sorted-before-dispatch failures that both builds show intermittently, and one seek-to-max-value failure in 7 runs with the series (`currentTime` 0 right after setting it), not reproduced in 6 reruns, apart from load-removes-queued-error-event, which now hangs whenever GStreamer 1.24.2's playbin3 stalls on a `<video>` with an audio-only MP3: 4 of 6 runs on this host without the series too; the stalled element delays the load event, and before, only a garbage collection that freed it let the load event fire (upstream's intermittent tracker has 9 unexpected results for that test in total). The three tests that fail on QuickJS CI pass with forced GCs every 20 ms too. With logging of the keep-alive updates, every element in direct checks stopped being kept alive once it had been loaded and never played, played to the end, paused, replayed after the end without a seekable range, or played backwards to the start. An independent agent review found no blockers; its two should-fix findings for this patch (ended but unpaused elements were kept alive; changes of the error went through no hook) are fixed. QuickJS release build of this stack: the new tests and the media tests that had QuickJS expectations (autoplay.html, allowed-to-play.html, resource-selection-candidate-moved.html and the three autoplay permissions policy tests) give SpiderMonkey's results in 2 of 2 runs; without the series they time out as the dropped expectations said.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; root-caused, written and tested by the agent)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
